# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Viljetech Platform — Backend environment variables
# Copy to `.env` and fill in local values. Never commit `.env`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Server
NODE_ENV=development                  # development | production | test
PORT=3001
LOG_LEVEL=debug                       # trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal

# CORS — comma-separated list of allowed origins
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reverse-proxy trust (BACKEND_AUDIT.md P2-13)
#
# Tells Express how many proxy hops to peel back when reading XFF.
# Wrong value → rate limiting, audit log, and per-user lockout all
# record the proxy IP instead of the client IP, OR a hostile client
# can spoof its IP via X-Forwarded-For.
#
#   0          = no proxy (local dev)
#   1          = single load balancer (default; matches App Service alone)
#   2          = Azure Front Door → App Service (typical production)
#   3          = Front Door → APIM → App Service
#   loopback   = trust only 127.0.0.1 / ::1 (sidecar proxy)
#   <CIDR>     = explicit allow-list, e.g. 10.0.0.0/8
#
# See `docs/deploy/trust-proxy-topology.md` for the full reasoning + a
# verification recipe to confirm the value matches reality post-deploy.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TRUST_PROXY=1

# Database
MONGO_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/viljetech

# JWT — plan-aligned TTLs (access 8h / refresh 5d).
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"
JWT_SECRET=change-me-use-a-long-random-string
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=8h
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=change-me-use-a-different-long-random-string
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=5d

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional: JWT key rotation via `kid` (BACKEND_AUDIT.md P0-3)
#
# Default mode: just `JWT_SECRET` is set; tokens carry no kid header.
# Rotation mode: register multiple secrets via `JWT_SECRET_<kid>` and
# point `JWT_ACTIVE_KID` at the one that signs new tokens. Verifier
# reads the kid header on incoming tokens and looks up the matching
# secret; tokens with no kid (issued pre-rotation) verify against the
# legacy `JWT_SECRET` for the access-TTL window.
#
# Rotation procedure:
#   1. Add JWT_SECRET_v2=<new>, JWT_ACTIVE_KID=v2, restart.
#   2. Wait max(access_ttl, refresh_ttl) for old tokens to expire.
#   3. Remove the old JWT_SECRET / JWT_SECRET_v1 entries, restart.
#
# Same convention for refresh: JWT_REFRESH_SECRET_<kid> + JWT_REFRESH_ACTIVE_KID.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JWT_SECRET_v1=...
# JWT_SECRET_v2=...
# JWT_ACTIVE_KID=v2
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET_v1=...
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET_v2=...
# JWT_REFRESH_ACTIVE_KID=v2

# MFA — base64-encoded 32-byte key used to AES-256-GCM encrypt TOTP secrets at rest.
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me-base64-32-bytes

# Issuer shown in authenticator apps (otpauth URL).
MFA_ISSUER=Viljetech

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional: MFA encryption-key rotation (BACKEND_AUDIT.md R16)
#
# Default mode: just `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY` is set; ciphertext format is
# `iv.ct.tag` (no kid prefix). Rotating the key would orphan every
# stored TOTP secret.
#
# Rotation mode: register multiple keys via `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_<kid>`
# and point `MFA_ENCRYPTION_ACTIVE_KID` at the active one. New
# encryptions stamp `kid:<kid>:` into the ciphertext blob; decrypt
# looks up the kid in the registry. Tokens written before rotation
# (no kid prefix) verify against the legacy `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY`.
#
# Rotation procedure:
#   1. Add MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_v2=<new>, MFA_ENCRYPTION_ACTIVE_KID=v2,
#      restart. New encryptions carry `kid:v2:`; legacy blobs still
#      decrypt under MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY.
#   2. Run a re-encryption pass: every `users.mfa_secret_encrypted`
#      gets read + re-encrypted under the active kid. Ship as a
#      one-off admin script / endpoint — not auto-migrated, the
#      operator decides timing.
#   3. Drop MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_v1 + the legacy MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY,
#      restart.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_v1=...
# MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_v2=...
# MFA_ENCRYPTION_ACTIVE_KID=v2

# Password reset — used to construct the link sent in reset emails.
# Final URL = ${APP_BASE_URL}${PASSWORD_RESET_URL_PATH}?token=<opaque>
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
PASSWORD_RESET_URL_PATH=/reset-password

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Azure Blob Storage — task attachments (Step 21) + profile photos (Step 22)
# All containers MUST be private. The API mints short-lived SAS tokens for
# upload + download; no public URLs are ever stored.
#
# Auth — provide ONE of the following:
#   1. AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING (preferred; one secret to rotate)
#   2. AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME + AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_KEY
#
# Both flavours require the account *key* (not just a SAS or managed identity)
# because SAS minting is performed server-side.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AZURE_STORAGE_CONNECTION_STRING=DefaultEndpointsProtocol=https;AccountName=...;AccountKey=...;EndpointSuffix=core.windows.net
# AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME=
# AZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_KEY=

# Container the task-attachments service writes to. Must already exist; the
# server does not auto-create production containers. Default matches
# Data_Model.md §5.6.
AZURE_BLOB_TASK_ATTACHMENTS_CONTAINER=task-attachments

# Container for user profile photos (Step 22). Same auth as task attachments;
# create the container ahead of time and keep it private — the API mints
# read SAS URLs on demand.
AZURE_BLOB_PROFILE_PHOTOS_CONTAINER=profile-photos

# Private container for generated payslip/letter PDFs (Payroll & Certificates
# foundation). Keep it private — downloads are always short-lived SAS URLs.
AZURE_BLOB_PAYROLL_DOCUMENTS_CONTAINER=payroll-documents

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Application Insights — log telemetry (V2-OPS-3)
#
# When set, every pino log line is shipped to Azure Application
# Insights as trace telemetry (errors also surface as exceptions in
# the "Failures" view). When unset, the logger behaves exactly as
# before — pino-pretty in dev, stdout JSON in prod — and the SDK is
# never even loaded.
#
# Get the value from: Azure Portal → your Application Insights
# resource → "Properties" → "Connection String". Format:
#   InstrumentationKey=…;IngestionEndpoint=…;LiveEndpoint=…;…
#
# Cost note: App Insights bills per GB of ingested logs. For an
# internal tool at v1 scale (~10s of employees), monthly ingestion
# is well under the 5 GB free tier. Set `LOG_LEVEL=info` (default
# in production) to keep volume modest — `debug` will inflate the
# bill quickly under sustained traffic.
# APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING=InstrumentationKey=...;IngestionEndpoint=https://...

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email transport (BACKEND_AUDIT.md P0-1)
#
# Selectable provider. Production MUST set this to `acs` or `mailjet`
# — leaving it at `stub` swallows all outbound mail (password resets,
# leave notifications, weekly digests, etc.) and only logs a warning.
#
#   stub    — dev/test default; logs the dispatch, no outbound delivery.
#   acs     — Azure Communication Services Email REST API.
#   mailjet — Mailjet v3.1 Send API.
#
# Boot fails loudly if `acs` or `mailjet` is selected without the
# matching credentials below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EMAIL_TRANSPORT=stub

# ── Azure Communication Services (when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=acs) ──
# `ACS_FROM_ADDRESS` must be a verified sender on the connected ACS
# resource (typically an `@<resource>.azurecomm.net` address or a
# domain you've provisioned + DKIM/SPF-verified inside ACS).
# ACS_CONNECTION_STRING=endpoint=https://<resource>.communication.azure.com/;accesskey=...
# ACS_FROM_ADDRESS=DoNotReply@<resource>.azurecomm.net

# ── Mailjet (when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=mailjet) ──
# Generate API key + secret at https://app.mailjet.com/account/apikeys
# `MAILJET_FROM_ADDRESS` must be a verified Mailjet sender.
# MAILJET_API_KEY=
# MAILJET_API_SECRET=
# MAILJET_FROM_ADDRESS=noreply@yourdomain.com
# MAILJET_FROM_NAME=Viljetech

# ── Report-subscription delivery override (demo/testing) ──
# When set, EVERY scheduled report-subscription email is delivered to
# this single address instead of each subscription's owner. The report
# data + audit target stay owner-scoped; only the recipient changes.
# Leave empty in normal operation. Change it to re-point demo emails.
# REPORT_SUBSCRIPTION_OVERRIDE_EMAIL=vivek@viljetech.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jobs / background work (F6 — formerly tracked under P3-19)
#
# `JOBS_ENABLED` is the master switch for the cron registry. The
# registry boots scheduled tasks (attendance finaliser, leave-year
# rollover, audit-log mirror, stuck-job sweeper, etc.) once the HTTP
# server is up. Off in tests + smoke runs so vitest doesn't fight
# the real schedules; on by default in dev / prod.
#
# M25 — multi-instance contract: on a scale-out deploy, set
# `JOBS_ENABLED=true` on EXACTLY ONE instance and `false` on every
# other. The DB unique index (`job_runs.{name, run_key}`) makes the
# data side idempotent regardless, but cron-fire bookkeeping + log
# volume + metric counters multiply per-instance otherwise. See
# `jobs/index.ts` startScheduler() comment for the long-form note.
#
# `EXPORT_WORKER_INLINE` is a test seam — when true, the report-export
# worker awaits the render synchronously instead of `setImmediate`-
# dispatching it. Tests use this to assert on the final state without
# timing fragility. Always false in production.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JOBS_ENABLED=true
# EXPORT_WORKER_INLINE=false

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PDF generation (F6 — formerly tracked under P3-19)
#
# `PDF_ENGINE` selects the PDF generator factory. Both options are
# bundled; pick based on output fidelity requirements:
#
#   pdfkit   — default. Fast, no external dependencies, good enough
#              for tabular reports (billing / utilization).
#   pdfmake  — declarative table renderer; richer styling but
#              slower for large reports.
#   puppet   — Puppeteer + headless Chrome. Highest fidelity
#              (full HTML/CSS), but requires a Chromium binary on
#              the host. Set `PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH` to point
#              at it when the bundled download isn't available
#              (e.g. Alpine images, tightened FROM-scratch builds).
#
# Both env vars are optional; absence falls back to `pdfkit` with
# the bundled defaults. Tests override `PDF_ENGINE` per-test to
# exercise the alternate factories.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PDF_ENGINE=pdfkit
# PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Slack
#
# IMPORTANT — the BOT TOKEN (xoxb-…) does NOT live here. Paste it
# into the Admin → Settings → Slack page in the web app instead.
# It's stored AES-encrypted in the database using MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY,
# so each environment / tenant can have its own token without a
# redeploy.
#
# The values below are for interactive Slack flows (button clicks,
# slash commands, modals, files.upload) — needed once the backend
# `/api/v1/slack/interactive` and `/api/v1/slack/commands/*` endpoints
# land. Safe to leave blank until then.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# From Slack app → Basic Information → App Credentials → Signing Secret.
# Used to verify the HMAC-SHA256 signature on every incoming Slack
# webhook request (X-Slack-Signature + X-Slack-Request-Timestamp).
# Without it, the interactive endpoints reject all calls.
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# From Slack app → Basic Information → App Credentials → App ID.
# Informational — logged on dispatch for traceability.
# SLACK_APP_ID=

# From Slack app → Basic Information → App Credentials → Client ID
# + Client Secret. Only needed when/if we add per-user Slack OAuth
# (e.g. "Connect your Slack account" on the profile page). Not
# required for the workspace-level bot integration.
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Web Push (V2-BE-10)
#
# Browser-side push notifications. Adds a 4th channel to the
# notification dispatcher (in-app + email + Slack + push). When
# the keys below are absent, push is silently disabled — the
# other three channels keep working.
#
# Generate keys with: `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`. The
# private key is server-only. The public key is also baked into
# the FE build via `VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` so the browser can
# generate a subscription this server will accept.
#
# `VAPID_SUBJECT` must be a `mailto:` URL or HTTPS URL. Used by
# the push provider as a contact point for delivery issues.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@yourcompany.com
